From fe5f75ceda84736e395a294260be8e402acd2fb7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Trung=20L=C3=AA?= <8@tle.id.au>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 00:26:47 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 28/30] Enable the Linux sandbox on ppc64

The seccomp-bpf layer gets the ppc64 register conventions: the syscall
number in r0, arguments in r3-r8 and the result in r3. While seccomp runs
the kernel has replaced r3 with -ENOSYS and keeps the first argument in
orig_gpr3, so the SIGSYS handler reads it from there. A syscall made with
sc reports an error through CR0.SO with a positive errno in r3, one made
with scv (which glibc uses on POWER9) returns -errno in r3, and the saved
trap slot says which instruction trapped, so the handler sets the result
in whichever convention applies. The raw syscall helper uses sc and folds
CR0.SO into -errno. Both byte orders are supported, with the BPF halfword
offsets swapped for big-endian.

The syscall table is generated from the powerpc asm/unistd_64.h, clone()
takes the CLONE_BACKWARDS argument order, and the direct System V IPC
syscalls are dispatched whenever a platform has them, since ppc64 has them
beside the ipc() multiplexer and glibc uses them.
